## Packages and Rust host functions

Use a source package for reusable Moth code and a host package for functions,
constants or opaque resources implemented outside Moth. Source packages give
the language-facing API its normal Moth ergonomics. Host packages keep the
foreign boundary small and explicit.

### Provide a source package

`SourcePackage::new` declares the package root and `from_dir` supplies its
installed source tree:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    let package = SourcePackage::new("@ui")
        .from_dir("installed-packages/ui")
        .available_in_templates();
]

Registration through `BuilderDefinition` records Builder provenance
automatically. The package still compiles under ordinary Moth package,
visibility and numeric-profile rules.

`available_in_templates` makes exported compile-time values eligible for the
builder's implicit template scope. It does not expose private declarations or
change Moth's no-shadowing rules.

### Declare a host function once

Apply `#[moth_host::host_function]` to a concrete Rust free function and
register the generated binding explicitly:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    #[moth_host::host_function]
    fn has_prefix(text: &str, prefix: &str) -> bool {
        text.starts_with(prefix)
    }

    fn platform_package() -> HostPackage<()> {
        HostPackage::stateless("@platform")
            .function(moth_host::host_binding!(has_prefix))
    }
]

The Rust function name is the Moth leaf name. There is no binding rename
facility. This keeps Rust and Moth searches aligned.

The package registration site owns the complete namespace hierarchy. Rust
module paths have no Moth namespace meaning:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    HostPackage::<GameHost>::new("@engine")
        .namespace("world", |world| {
            world.function(moth_host::host_binding!(spawn));
            world.function(moth_host::host_binding!(destroy));
        })
        .namespace("audio", |audio| {
            audio.function(moth_host::host_binding!(play));
        })
]

The attribute preserves the Rust function and generates a typed descriptor and
runtime adapter. `host_binding!` selects that generated binding. It does not
scan the crate, register a global plugin, generate Moth source or create an HTTP
endpoint.

One package declaration function is the Rust authority used by both
`moth-builder` during compilation and `moth-host` at runtime.

### Host signature reference

The automatic profile is intentionally closed. An accepted Rust form has one
documented Moth meaning. Unsupported forms produce diagnostics that explain
what to use instead.

[table:
    [th:
        [: Moth form]
        [: Rust host signature]
        [: Notes]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `bool`]
        [: Parameter and result.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `Char`]
        [: `char`]
        [: Parameter and result.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `I8`, `I16`, `I32`, `I64`]
        [: `i8`, `i16`, `i32`, `i64`]
        [: Exact fixed-width parameter and result types.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `U8`, `U16`, `U32`, `U64`]
        [: `u8`, `u16`, `u32`, `u64`]
        [: Exact fixed-width parameter and result types.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `F32`, `F64`]
        [: `f32`, `f64`]
        [: Exact fixed-width parameter and result types.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `Byte`]
        [: `moth_host::Byte`]
        [: Nominal octet type. Rust `u8` always means Moth `U8`.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `Int` parameter]
        [: `i32` or `i64` when the selected profile fits losslessly]
        [: `i32` accepts `Int` only under Int32. `i64` accepts Int32 or Int64. No Rust result type implicitly returns `Int`.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `Float` parameter]
        [: `f32` or `f64` when the selected profile fits losslessly]
        [: `f32` accepts `Float` only under Float32. `f64` accepts Float32 or Float64. No Rust result type implicitly returns `Float`.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `String` parameter]
        [: `&str`]
        [: Shared call-scoped text input. The host cannot retain the borrow.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `String` result]
        [: `String`]
        [: Becomes independent Moth-owned string data.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `T?`]
        [: `Option<T>`]
        [: One optional layer over an otherwise eligible boundary type.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Zero success returns]
        [: `()`]
        [: Ordinary successful completion with no value slot.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Multiple success returns]
        [: `(T1, T2, ...)` as the outer return shape]
        [: Ordered Moth result slots, not a Moth tuple. Tuple parameters and nested tuples are invalid.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Built-in `Error!`]
        [: `Result<T, moth_host::Error>` as the outer return shape]
        [: The only automatic Rust error channel. `T` may be `()`, one result, an optional or a multiple-return tuple.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Opaque host type]
        [: A marker declared with `#[moth_host::host_type]`]
        [: Runtime-scoped typed handle. Pass the marker by value.]
    ]
]

`F16`, `DecN`, collections, maps, arbitrary structs, choices, callbacks and
other function values have no automatic Rust host representation. Convert
explicitly in Moth or expose an opaque host resource when identity rather than
value structure is the real boundary.

Fixed-width Moth numerics do not implicitly widen to another fixed-width Rust
parameter. An `i64` parameter accepts `I64`, not `I32`. The extra compatibility
for `Int` and `Float` exists only because their widths are selected by the
builder's one `NumericProfile`.

Fixed-width Rust results remain fixed-width Moth results. `i64 -> Int` and
`f64 -> Float` are not inferred from the selected profile.

`String` parameters are rejected because they would imply an unnecessary
ownership transfer. Borrowed string results are rejected because a Rust borrow
cannot escape into Moth. `Option<&str>` is valid only in parameter position and
`Option<String>` only in result position.

Nested `Option`, nested `Result`, arbitrary Rust error types, tuple parameters
and nested tuple returns are rejected.

Host functions are synchronous. Rust `async fn` is rejected with a diagnostic
that points to Moth's async boundary rather than treating a `Future` as an
ordinary result. Async host calls extend this profile only when Moth's own async
semantics define suspension, cancellation and lifetime behaviour.

Architecture-dependent integers such as `usize` and `isize`, raw pointers,
`Box<T>`, `Vec<T>`, slices other than `&str`, arrays, standard maps, trait
objects, `impl Trait`, generic functions, unsafe functions and foreign-ABI
functions are also rejected. Diagnostics should name the compatible form when
one exists.

### Numeric parameter compatibility

The compiler, not the proc macro, decides whether a profile-selected numeric
argument can use a fixed Rust carrier. The descriptor records the Rust carrier
and its permitted native compatibility. The active `NumericProfile` completes
the check.

This keeps the mapping deterministic across all four Int/Float profiles and
prevents the macro from defining a second numeric type system.

### Host types are typed handles

Declare an opaque host resource as a marker:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    #[moth_host::host_type]
    pub struct Entity;
]

The marker is not the underlying Rust resource. `moth-host` represents it with
a runtime-scoped typed handle. The actual Bevy entity, database key, renderer
resource or other payload remains host-owned.

A `moth-host` runtime owns one canonical handle table. Handles carry enough
identity to reject:

- a handle for another host type
- a stale handle whose slot has been reused
- a handle created by another runtime instance

The table stores host-defined payloads rather than taking ownership of the
external resource itself.

Copying a Moth host value copies the handle identity. It does not clone the
underlying resource.

Opaque host values always support `is` and `is not`. Equality compares the
complete handle identity, including runtime and generation identity, even when
the resource later becomes stale. It never invokes `PartialEq` on the
underlying Rust payload.

Opaque host types gain no ordering, arithmetic, hashing or map-key support.
Those behaviours require explicit package functions or Moth wrappers.

### Shared and exclusive host-resource access

A host marker is passed by value in Rust. Rust `&T` and `&mut T` do not describe
Moth access to the resource behind a handle.

Use `#[moth(exclusive)]` when the Moth caller must supply exclusive access:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    #[moth_host::host_function]
    fn destroy(
        #[moth(host)] host: &mut GameHost,
        #[moth(exclusive)] entity: Entity,
    ) -> Result<(), moth_host::Error> {
        let payload = host.resources_mut().remove(entity)?;
        host.world.destroy(payload);
        Ok(())
    }
]

Without `#[moth(exclusive)]`, an opaque host parameter uses shared Moth access.
The access mode is part of the binding contract and participates in normal Moth
borrow validation.

Handle validation happens before user Rust code receives the binding call. A
fallible binding can report an invalid or stale handle through its declared
`Error!` channel. An infallible binding has no hidden Moth error channel, so a
handle validation failure is a host-boundary fault rather than an invented
success value.

### One host environment per runtime domain

A host package is parameterised by one concrete host-environment type:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    use moth_host::{HostEnvironment, HostResources};

    struct GameHost {
        resources: HostResources,
        world: GameWorld,
    }

    impl HostEnvironment for GameHost {
        fn resources(&self) -> &HostResources {
            &self.resources
        }

        fn resources_mut(&mut self) -> &mut HostResources {
            &mut self.resources
        }
    }
]

A binding may request zero or one host-provided environment parameter. When
present it is first, explicitly marked `#[moth(host)]`, and typed as `&Env` or
`&mut Env`:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    #[moth_host::host_function]
    fn frame_count(
        #[moth(host)] host: &GameHost,
    ) -> u64 {
        host.world.frame_count()
    }
]

The host environment is not part of the Moth-visible signature. It is an
embedding facility, not a Moth ambient context, allocator, error handler,
dynamic scope or dependency-injection system.

If a host needs several services, its environment type composes them. Bindings
cannot request arbitrary service types independently.

A `HostPackage<GameHost>` accepts bindings whose host parameter is
`GameHost`, plus stateless bindings. Another runtime domain can use a different
environment type. `BuilderDefinition` consumes a type-erased semantic
projection and does not become generic over every runtime domain.

### Host constants

Host packages may register immutable compile-time scalar constants explicitly:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    HostPackage::<GameHost>::new("@engine")
        .constant("max_players", 64_u32)
]

A zero-argument Rust function is still a function. It never becomes a constant
implicitly.

Host constants use the closed supported scalar profile. Runtime-dependent
values, opaque handles, strings, collections and structured values are not
host constants.

### Runtime faults are not Moth errors

`Result<T, moth_host::Error>` is expected recoverable failure. A Rust panic,
unexpected runtime trap or adapter contract violation is a host fault.

When Rust unwinding is enabled, `moth-host` contains panics at the binding
dispatch boundary so they do not unwind through Wasm/runtime machinery. It does
not convert them to `Error!`. With `panic = "abort"`, Rust's abort policy
remains authoritative.

After a host fault that may have left environment state inconsistent, the host
must not resume the affected invocation as though the binding returned
normally.

### Build-time and runtime contract identity

Every registered host function has one stable semantic identity and one
contract fingerprint derived from package path, namespace, symbol, parameter
types/access, ordered success results and error channel.

Rust addresses, crate versions and compiler-local IDs are not semantic
identity.

The compiler records the exact reachable host contracts required by an
artefact. Before application code executes, `moth-host` validates those
requirements against the installed package declarations. A renamed function,
changed result, changed access mode or changed error channel cannot link merely
because its physical Wasm carriers still happen to fit.
